ing  the video of his brother  being killed by a minneapolis police  officer  he gave voice to the pain that the  entire nation felt  he also spoke to the anger of knowing  that george floyd was only the latest  in a much too long list of victims of  police misconduct  disproportionately people of color he  spoke to the frustration  that time and again in the face of  overwhelming evidence that dramatic  reform is needed  congress has done very little mr floyd  charged us with making sure that the  death of his brother  and other victims of police violence  would not be in vain  and he pleaded with us to turn that pain  and anger that we all feel  into meaningful change his words echoed  the voices of millions of americans  who demand justice and demand action  today we answer their call and as we  answer their call  i want to comment on a few of the  uh contentions of the republicans in  opposing this bill  they say that it would defund the police  no it wouldn't  it will clearly not defund the police  that is not the intent of the bill nor  the effect of it  all you have to do is read it secondly  they say they're very upset  at the repeal of qualified immunity in  the bill  but you know qualified immunity and they  talk as if  revealing qualified immunity would make  the police  um victims of of all kinds of things  but the fact is that qualified immunity  is a judge-made  doctrine 40 years ago you didn't have it  judicial qualified immunity was invented  by the courts  only in the last 40 years and 40 years  ago  you had the same problems but you didn't  have police officers  being drawn and courted or losing their  homes  and all these other things that the  republicans  would make you fear so the legislation  is very carefully drafted  it's timely it's for the moment  and it is very very necessary and i  thank  the uh um i thank the leadership of the  house  for um for supporting this bill  the honor to introduce uh the majority  leader of the house  mr hoyer 